Every position vector starts at one special place: the **origin** - your "home base," wherever you decide to put it (0, 0). It always starts there and points straight to wherever the object currently is. {{viz:anim-01}}

**Writing It Down** {{viz:formula-01}}
If an object sits at coordinates (x, y), its position vector r is:

r = x î + y ĵ

Same idea as any other vector - it's just that this particular one is anchored at the origin by definition. You never draw a position vector starting anywhere else.

**Why Not Just Use Coordinates?**
Fair question - (x, y) already tells you where something is. But writing it as a vector r lets you do vector math with position: add displacement to it, take derivatives to get velocity, and so on. Physics runs on vector operations, so position needs to speak that language too. {{viz:chart-01}}

**Position Changes = Displacement**
Here's where it gets useful: if an object moves from position r₁ to position r₂, the **displacement** is just the difference: Δr = r₂ - r₁. That's a brand-new vector pointing from the old spot straight to the new one - not the path taken, just start to finish. {{viz:3d-01}}

**A Quick Mental Model**
Imagine tracking a drone with GPS. At any moment, its position vector is the arrow from your launch point (the origin) to wherever the drone is right now. As the drone flies around, that arrow's length and direction keep changing - but it always starts at the same spot: home. {{viz:text-01}} That's the whole idea locked in: position vector = arrow from origin to "here, right now." {{viz:simulation-01}}
